#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645a7e0b-2e3d-5867-b381-6a1443ab619a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411437} {:answer/id #uuid "87d10ffd-ae37-597e-8619-29681e2d4321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411442} {:answer/id #uuid "cc8968ee-c0f9-5a40-a746-8ba2c518a9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411451} {:answer/id #uuid "cf0e0014-393a-5d8a-a0b7-5361c1d979fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411452} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e73c4f-2431-51f3-9280-279505b4a83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87d10ffd-ae37-597e-8619-29681e2d4321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411442} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f12865-3f75-50a9-82af-d153989de8e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8d1da1f-2237-5249-8b88-e74004bb86c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94985" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}=10?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411460 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24fa485f-91ea-5bd7-b5bf-ff53f7921b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A  hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411433} {:answer/id #uuid "40462880-f937-54f8-9c63-a892cafb32da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes  $2$ and $\\sqrt{8}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411435} {:answer/id #uuid "69cb5485-7267-5d3c-8e9b-6410002fdbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes  $\\sqrt{8}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411438} {:answer/id #uuid "a1267461-8286-578d-8e73-1197cd603858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle with radius  $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411446} {:answer/id #uuid "efcf49e3-8778-553a-9158-db5800030dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24fa485f-91ea-5bd7-b5bf-ff53f7921b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A  hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411433} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdaee3e-396b-5d40-981f-615ed5526c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6abf962b-d107-56b7-9263-80678639c14a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95002" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}=32?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+10{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.  \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -16 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =8,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2} & =32 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes  $2$ and $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "225ec156-2776-5590-b42c-ad1225863eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411432} {:answer/id #uuid "84e45c48-61da-5ecf-bca7-7e74358b27b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411441} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0771fd-b582-50d2-908c-56e97b6c3960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411443} {:answer/id #uuid "97642761-88ff-5804-a356-24637c6c8d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411445} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b9b25d-c9bb-564c-9102-d912c2e151d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "225ec156-2776-5590-b42c-ad1225863eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411432} :answer-field/id #uuid "b632b82e-8e97-55cb-a0fa-eef141821277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9ebdd12-b59c-52db-97fa-216d502d55a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95042" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve ${x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}=40.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+12{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=40\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ \n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)1I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-8)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162011 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c050d04-cc57-5e4f-9981-5ac992758bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411430} {:answer/id #uuid "352606af-80ab-5092-8c74-0764cad932bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411434} {:answer/id #uuid "72d0140b-00e2-525a-ace5-a15c912b0d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411439} {:answer/id #uuid "a251106f-0d50-5a25-bdd3-04521faa8cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411447} {:answer/id #uuid "ba35334a-83ae-5683-b824-2a6a574c3bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba35334a-83ae-5683-b824-2a6a574c3bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411448} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf54027-1555-5e76-9f5a-c93113c1e7f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec22f795-26e2-5bc9-b423-bed373ca22ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-95809" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve ${x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=48.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=48\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162044 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15485fac-790e-5582-90f5-2eb0336ce86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411431} {:answer/id #uuid "51d9a677-8641-50b7-bf65-1ab6b97a0b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{11} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411436} {:answer/id #uuid "783c1108-45c5-5c61-b1de-135da5ea10e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411440} {:answer/id #uuid "96370f14-3c4b-563e-82b2-3cc6d00f55b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411444} {:answer/id #uuid "c141d751-a196-5cd6-b4cd-6bb466dd2ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96370f14-3c4b-563e-82b2-3cc6d00f55b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411444} :answer-field/id #uuid "de22cbdd-1887-52e7-a256-745226952983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "611baa25-d6cc-5c6f-9064-69e0865e9f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97986" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
